{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>But if on the day that her husband hears he nullifies it, etc., and YHWH will forgive her.</b> That is to say, regarding the father's nullification — since she is under his authority, she requires his nullification; for he already stated 'in her youth, in her father's house,' meaning: all the time she is in her youth she is under her father's authority. A married woman — that is what is written 'and if she is in her husband's house, her vow,' and the husband may nullify his wife's vows from the time she enters the bridal canopy until she reaches adulthood; but vows she took before she came under his authority, he has no power to nullify — and this is what they said of blessed memory: 'the husband does not nullify those that preceded.' And since every woman who vows, vows with her husband's knowledge in mind, Scripture treated her as though it were explicitly stipulated with him, on condition that her husband be willing, for the sake of domestic peace; and even in a Torah-level vow and rabbinic-level marriage he may nullify for this reason — for example, a girl of twelve years who has no father and knows for whose name she vowed, and her mother or brother married her off. A widow — that is what is written 'and the vow of a widow and a divorcée' — a widow from full marriage (nissu'in), for if from betrothal (erusin) she would return to the father's authority; but once he married her, her father no longer has authority over her. And from here is derived that the bridal canopy confers by Torah law: the right to inherit her, and to become impure for her if he is a priest, and to nullify her vows alone, and that the labor of her hands belongs to him."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:37:04.526989322Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}